seoContent: |
  <p>HTML encoding converts special characters into HTML entities so they display safely instead of being read as markup. It's the difference between a comment box that shows a user's text and one that lets that text execute as code.</p>
  <p>Consider a comment containing <em>&lt;script&gt;alert('x')&lt;/script&gt;</em>. Left unencoded, a browser reads that as an instruction. Encoded, it displays as harmless text — this single transformation closes off most Cross-Site Scripting attempts.</p>
  <h3>Where it matters beyond security</h3>
  <p>Mathematical comparisons like "5 &lt; 10" can silently break page layout if the angle bracket isn't encoded. Quotes inside HTML attributes can terminate a string early. Encoding keeps both the visual output and the underlying markup intact at the same time.</p>
  <p>International text adds another layer — accented letters, CJK characters, and symbols outside the basic ASCII range all need consistent entity handling to render the same way across browsers and devices.</p>
  <h3>How this tool handles it</h3>
  <p>Everything runs client-side: the five reserved characters are escaped first, then, if enabled, remaining non-ASCII characters convert to named or numeric entities. Nothing typed here is ever uploaded anywhere.</p>
  <p>For most people this tool gets used for a few seconds at a time — paste, encode, copy, done. That's the whole job: get out of the way, and be correct every time.</p>
